{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>The Midianite woman.</b> It says it with the definite article heh. It would appear that there was no other Midianite woman in this promiscuity (mufkarut) besides her, and therefore also in parashat Balak (Numbers 25:6), when it mentions ״וַיַּקְרֵב אֶל אֶחָיו״, it says ״אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִית״ with the definite article heh, to indicate that specific Midianite woman who was in the promiscuity. Accordingly, the men of Midian did not profer their daughters, and if the Midianite women had been in promiscuity, HaShem would have commanded concerning them to kill them. And so you will find that when the verse says (Numbers 25:1): ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם לִזְנוֹת אֶל בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב״ — this teaches you that the ones in promiscuity were Moabite women. And the reason the men of Midian did not profer their daughters is because they were not as confident in them as the Moabite women were, as I wrote above (Numbers 24:14) on the verse ״אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה וְגוֹ׳ בְּאַחֲרִית הַיָּמִים״. And as for this one who went out — her reason was at her side, for she was the daughter of Balak, the great enemy who hired Balaam son of Beor against us and accepted his advice and proferred his daughter — whereas the men of Midian, small and great, did not profer." "<b>And with this</b> I found satisfaction in the statement of Hazal (Numbers Rabbah 21:3; Tanḥuma, Mattot 4) who said that Zur, who was killed with the princes of Midian, is Balak, and because he proffered his daughter, they demoted him from his greatness — for he had been the head of them all, as it is written ״רֹאשׁ אֻמּוֹת״ — and now they counted him third among the kings of Midian. Now one needs to know: who revealed this secret, that Zur is Balak? According to our words there is a sound reason to say so, for since we find only one Midianite woman here, the intelligent reader asks: what was different about this Midianite woman, that she stood out from all the daughters of Midian? Rather, it is certainly because this is Balak, who hired Balaam against us, and took his advice, and carried out his counsel through his daughter. And he is Zur, who degraded his own honor in order to carry out the evil counsel of Balaam. Or perhaps Moab did not wish to profer their daughters until Balak — who was then king over them — took the initiative and proffered his daughter. And even though I wrote above that he did not retain his kingship after Balaam informed them that they could neither harm nor destroy Moab, immediately he carried out the counsel and went his way to his city, and through this Moab proffered their daughters; and without this, how could it be that Zur alone would be different from all the rest?" "<b>However,</b> a midrash came into my hands (Yalkut, Mattot), and this is its language: ״הֵן הֵנָּה״ teaches that they would recognize them and say to one another: this is the one with whom so-and-so sinned — end quote. According to this midrash, it was not one or two [women]. And it is possible to say that Midian did not profer the daughters of their great men but only those of the common people, and therefore they demoted Balak, who proffered his daughter, and he had been a king. According to this approach, <b>הַמִּדְיָנִית</b> means the most distinguished of all of them, for no king's daughter went out from Midian except her. And according to this approach, what I explained at the end of parashat Balak will be confirmed for us — that Israel did not recognize them, for there were many Midianite women there, and the verse said (Numbers 25:1) only ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם לִזְנוֹת אֶל בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב״ — and why did it not also mention the daughters of Midian? This teaches you that in their eyes they were all daughters of Moab, for otherwise they would have killed them first and taken their property, or at the very least, after the deed of Phinehas, they would have had to kill the daughters of Midian — but all the women were under the presumption of being daughters of Moab." "<b>In Midian, he.</b> This statement is difficult in its meaning. If the intent is to say that he was the head of the nations of Midian, it should have said ״רֹאשׁ וְגוֹ׳ בֵּית אָב לְמִדְיָן״, for the construal of the word ״בְּמִדְיָן״ conveys that it informs us of his place, and the intent behind this is not known. It would appear that the intent is as follows: since it calls Zur ״רֹאשׁ אֻמּוֹת״ for Balak, the reader would say — but was not Balak king of Moab, whom they had appointed over them? Therefore the verse informs you and says: not as you think, that he remained king over Moab; rather, he had already been expelled from them and returned to his own place. And that is what it says <b>בְּמִדְיָן הוּא</b> — meaning, in that state of affairs, when his daughter was being proffered, he was in Midian. And see what I explained on the verse (Numbers 24:25) ״וְגַם בָּלָק הָלַךְ״ וְגוֹ׳:"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:32.197217498Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}